{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,30]],"date-time":"2026-10-30T21:38:03Z","timestamp":1793396283590,"version":"4.5.0"},"reference-count":102,"publisher":"American Association for the Advancement of Science (AAAS)","issue":"6334","funder":[{"DOI":"10.13039\/100000104","name":"National Aeronautics and Space Administration","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","award":["award277677"],"award-info":[{"award-number":["award277677"]}],"id":[{"id":"10.13039\/100000104","id-type":"DOI","asserted-by":"publisher"}]}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["Science"],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2017,4,14]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Hydrothermal processes on Enceladus<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>\n                    Saturn's moon Enceladus has a subsurface ocean covered by a layer of ice. Some liquid escapes into space through cracks in the ice, which is the source of one of Saturn's rings. In October 2015, the Cassini spacecraft flew directly through the plume of escaping material and sampled its chemical composition. Waite\n                    <jats:italic>et al.<\/jats:italic>\n                    found that the plume contains molecular hydrogen, H\n                    <jats:sub>2<\/jats:sub>\n                    , a sign that the water in Enceladus' ocean is reacting with rocks through hydrothermal processes (see the Perspective by Seewald). This drives the ocean out of chemical equilibrium, in a similar way to water around Earth's hydrothermal vents, potentially providing a source of chemical energy.\n                  <\/jats:p>\n                  <jats:p>\n                    <jats:italic>Science<\/jats:italic>\n                    , this issue p.\n                    <jats:related-article xmlns:xlink=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1999\/xlink\" ext-link-type=\"doi\" issue=\"6334\" page=\"155\" related-article-type=\"in-this-issue\" vol=\"356\" xlink:href=\"10.1126\/science.aai8703\">155<\/jats:related-article>\n                    ; see also p.\n                    <jats:related-article xmlns:xlink=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1999\/xlink\" ext-link-type=\"doi\" issue=\"6334\" page=\"132\" related-article-type=\"in-this-issue\" vol=\"356\" xlink:href=\"10.1126\/science.aan0444\">132<\/jats:related-article>\n                  <\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1126\/science.aai8703","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2017,4,13]],"date-time":"2017-04-13T13:55:16Z","timestamp":1492091716000},"page":"155-159","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":579,"title":["Cassini finds molecular hydrogen in the Enceladus plume: Evidence for hydrothermal processes"],"prefix":"10.1126","volume":"356","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-1978-1025","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"J. Hunter","family":"Waite","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Space Science and Engineering Division, Southwest Research Institute, 6220 Culebra Road, San Antonio, TX 78238, USA."},{"name":"Department of Physics and Astronomy, University of Texas, San Antonio, TX 78249, USA."}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2161-4672","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Christopher R.","family":"Glein","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Space Science and Engineering Division, Southwest Research Institute, 6220 Culebra Road, San Antonio, TX 78238, USA."}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-1858-4692","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Rebecca S.","family":"Perryman","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Space Science and Engineering Division, Southwest Research Institute, 6220 Culebra Road, San Antonio, TX 78238, USA."}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-8386-6413","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ben D.","family":"Teolis","sequence